+### 2300 — Sibling terms hide a clause the same way preceding lines do
+- ROOT CAUSE: `equalProjectionSchemaEntry` in `internal/localstore/projection.go` compares four identity fields in one `||` chain. Every existing drift fixture changed the SQL text or the `(type, name)` key, so a sibling term always decided the outcome first and the `actual.table != expected.table` term was reachable, exercised and pinned by nothing. Deleting only that term left the entire `localstore` suite green — reproduced before fixing.
+- FINDING: This is the round-5 shape-clause defect in a different syntactic shape. There the check that decided instead of the target was the *next line*; here it is a *sibling term in the same expression*. The isolation rule generalises: a negative fixture must leave every other decider matching, whether that decider sits above the target or beside it.
+- FINDING: SQLite accepts `PRAGMA writable_schema=ON; UPDATE sqlite_master SET tbl_name='projection_metadata' WHERE type='index' AND sql IS NULL` and reopens the database. Every other sqlite_master rewrite tried in this package fails as `malformed database schema`. That makes the moved autoindex `tbl_name` the one drift class no other comparison term can observe, and the only fixture that isolates this term.
+- DECISION: Extend `readProjectionSchemaShape` with per-field maps (`names`, `tables`, `hasStatement`) rather than reuse its `keys` field. `keys` embeds the table name, so it cannot express "this term changed and those did not" — the assertion the anti-vacuity guard needs.
