+### 2245 — Reaching a refusal clause is not pinning it
+- ROOT CAUSE: Every fixture for a *shape* clause in `internal/localstore/projection.go` — the checks that refuse a path because it is not the kind of file it must be — was a directory at mode `0700` or a symlink at lstat permission `0777`. Both are refused by the `verifyOwnerFileInfo(info, 0o600)` call on the following line, so the shape clause was reached but never decided anything the suite could observe. Five clauses survived individual disablement; the blob-leaf pair survived being disabled *together*.
+- FINDING: A derived refusal inventory can prove a site was reached. It cannot prove the site decided the outcome. Those are different facts, and positive-path-only coverage of a refusal looks identical to real coverage in both the inventory and the mutant count until the fixture is built so that every preceding check passes.
+- FINDING: The isolating fixture for this whole family is one thing — a FIFO created at exactly mode `0600`. Not a symlink, owned by the effective user, already owner-only, so only the shape clause can refuse it. It had been written once, for the lock file, and not applied to any sibling.
+- ANOMALY: The blob-leaf regularity clause bounds availability, not only safety. `os.Open` and SQLite block indefinitely on a FIFO with no writer, so removing the clause converts a refusal into an unbounded hang inside `OpenProjection`. A negative case that calls the entry point inline would hang the suite instead of reddening it; the case must assert a bounded return. Mutant `m11` reddens after 20.04s at that bound.
+- DECISION: Where an isolating fixture exists, pin the clause rather than annotate it `projection-refusal-subsumed`. The shard-kind clause was provably subsumed by `verifyOwnerDirectory` and was still worth pinning with a 0600 FIFO shard, because subsumption is an argument and a fixture is evidence.
+- DECISION: `unix.Flock` mutual exclusion was advertised in `README.md` and pinned by nothing — the 6-worker convergence test passes on SQLite's `busy_timeout` alone. flock arbitrates *open file descriptions*, so holding one in-process reproduces a second process exactly; no subprocess needed.
